<Article slug="reading-the-surface">
  <p>
    A soda-fired pot carries a record of its own firing. Once you know a few of the marks, you can
    pick up a cup and see which side faced the flame, where the soda landed thickest, and what was
    sitting next to it in the kiln.
  </p>

  <h2>Flashing</h2>
  <p>
    Flashing is the warm colour that bare clay takes on where flame and soda vapour touch it:
    peach, rust, copper and amber, fading to pale where the pot was shielded. The same white
    stoneware can come out bone pale on one side and deep orange on the other. That colour is what
    I call <a href="/surfaces/">Ember</a>. Digitalfire, a long-running ceramics reference, has a
    good <a href="https://digitalfire.com/glossary/flashing" rel="noopener">technical page on
    flashing</a> if you want the chemistry.
  </p>

  <h2>Orange peel</h2>
  <p>
    Where the soda lands heavily, it melts into a thin glaze with a dimpled texture, a lot like
    the skin of an orange. Run a finger over the shoulder of a pot and you can often feel it. It is
    a sign the soda reached that spot and reacted with the clay, and it only happens in salt and
    soda kilns. More on that in the
    <a href="https://digitalfire.com/glossary/orange+peel" rel="noopener">Digitalfire glossary</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Shadows and directions</h2>
  <p>
    The flame moves through the kiln in one direction, so most pots have a front and a back. The
    side facing the burners usually shows more colour and more soda, while the far side stays
    quieter. A neighbouring pot can leave a soft "shadow" where it blocked the flow. These are not
    flaws. They are the reason no two pieces from the same firing match.
  </p>

  <h2>Glaze breaking over edges</h2>
  <p>
    Where a glaze runs thin over a ridge, a carved line or a <a href="/learn/faceting/">facet</a>,
    it "breaks" lighter and lets the clay or flashing show through. Where it gathers in a hollow, it
    pools darker. Look at the edges of an <a href="/collection/?surface=oribe">Oribe</a> or
    <a href="/collection/?surface=tidepool">Tidepool</a> piece and you will see both at once.
  </p>

  <h2>Marks on the foot</h2>
  <p>
    Pots in a soda kiln sit on small pads of refractory clay, called wadding, so the melting soda
    does not glue them to the shelf. When the wads are knocked off after firing they leave small
    round marks on the foot. Next to them you will find my MM, carved into the clay by hand before
    the piece goes in.
  </p>

